STATE SYNTAX

When designing MACH355/435/445/465 state machines, you should not
rely on power-up reset to establish a start-up state.  Asynchronous
initialization product term allocation may result in a start-up state 
other than the one specified.  For this reason, the START_UP 
command is not supported by the MACHXL software state machine
syntax.

In order to set up a known starting state, you must do the following 
when using state machine syntax:

        1. define the initial state; and
        2. define the initialization transition.

1. Defining the initial state

The state machine needs to know which state bit values you intend as 
the initial state.  If you do not specify this, the software will
assign them  for you, and they might not agree with what you want.
This is done by defining the state bit pattern for at least the
initial state using the following syntax:

INITIAL_STATE = BIT0*/BIT1*/BIT2*... ;AS APPROP

This syntax is defined in the MACHXL Software User's Manual.

2. Defining the initialization transition

There are a number of ways you can do this.  You can design either 
synchronous or asynchronous initialization.  In the synchronous case, 
you can use either the state machine section or boolean equations to 
establish the starting state.  Asynchronous initialization uses boolean 
equations.

Synchronous initialization

Synchronous initialization provides a reset that is synchronized with 
the state machine clock.  Once the reset input condition is active, the 
initialization will occur on the next clock edge.  The practical 
implications of this are that when you power up, you need to assert the 
reset condition and clock the device in order to start out in the right 
state.

The most rigorous way to build initialization into your state machine 
is to include it explicitly in the state machine definition.  This means 
that for every state, a transition should be provided back to the initial 
state.  This transition is to be taken if the reset input condition exists.  
All other transitions should be qualified to make sure they donÕt occur 
when the reset input condition is active.  In addition, the best solution 
is to define all unused states with a reset transition to ensure correct 
initialization out of any illegal states.

As an example, suppose we have the following simple three-state 
machine, implemented with three of the four combinations of two 
state bits:

STATE

STATE1 := IN1 -> STATE2
        +-> STATE3

STATE2 := VCC -> STATE3

STATE3 := VCC -> STATE1

Given a reset input called RST, and assuming we want our initial state 
bit value to be [00], this machine can be modified as follows:

STATE

STATE1 = /Q0*/Q1  ;DEFINE THE INITIAL STATE BITS

STATE1 := COND1 -> STATE2
        + RST -> STATE1	;ADD RESET TRANSITION
        +-> STATE3

STATE2 := RST -> STATE1   ;ADD RESET TRANSITION
        +-> STATE3

STATE3 := VCC -> STATE1   ;NO EXTRA TRANSITION NEEDED

ILLEGAL1 := VCC -> STATE1  ;BEST, OR RST -> STATE1

CONDITION

COND1 = IN1*/RST          ;THIS TRANSITION ONLY IF NOT RESETING

Another possibility is to add some boolean equations following the 
state machine section.  This requires the use of curly brackets ({}),
since you will be modifying equations that the software will generate
from the state machine section; you wonÕt know what those equations
will look like when you are writing the equations.

Assuming you are using Q0 and Q1 to implement the state bits, and 
you wanted [00] as the initial state, you could solve the equations above 
in the following way.

STATE

STATE1 = /Q0*/Q1  ;DEFINE THE INITIAL STATE BITS

STATE1 := IN1 -> STATE2
        +-> STATE3

STATE2 := VCC -> STATE3

STATE3 := VCC -> STATE1

   ;WOULD STILL BE GOOD TO DEFINE ILLEGAL STATE

EQUATIONS

;AND ALL PRODUCT TERMS WITH /RST IF 0 DESIRED

Q0 := {Q0}*/RST
Q1 := {Q1}*/RST

   ;ADD RST PRODUCT TERM IF 1 WERE DESIRED

   ;Q0 := {Q0}
   ;    + RST

Asynchronous initialization

In this case, the asynchronous initializati
on product terms are used, so 
that as soon as the reset condition is active, the intialization will occur 
without waiting for the clock.

Boolean equations are simply written to define the initial values, as 
shown below.

STATE

STATE1 = /Q0*/Q1  ;DEFINE THE INITIAL STATE BITS

STATE1 := IN1 -> STATE2
        +-> STATE3

STATE2 := VCC -> STATE3

STATE3 := VCC -> STATE1

   ;WOULD STILL BE GOOD TO DEFINE ILLEGAL STATE

EQUATIONS

;AND ALL PRODUCT TERMS WITH /RST

Q0.RSTF := RST   ;OR SETF IF A 1 IS DESIRED
Q1.RSTF := RST   ;OR SETF IF A 1 IS DESIRED


